  "seoBody": "<p>Read this as a timeline. The earliest projects sit at the top and the most recent sits at the bottom, showing how repeated admin, reporting, approvals, and awkward business workflows gradually pushed the business toward workflow systems.</p><p>2025 to 2026 timeline. 6 systems delivered. Most recent at the bottom.</p><h2>These projects matter because they show repeated operational drag in the real world.</h2><p>They are not all the same product. They do show the same pattern: useful work gets slowed down by repeated write-ups, awkward handoffs, approvals, and manual admin sitting between the job and the finished output. That pattern is what later shaped the quickler workflow systems direction.</p><h3><a href=\"/pages/project-fundraising-charity.html\">Fundraising automation</a></h3><p>Nov 2025 · Charitable enterprise. One of the first sharp proofs that repeat-heavy documents could be drafted much faster when the right source information was already structured. Build: 2 hours. Saves: 6.5 days/year.</p><h3><a href=\"/pages/project-production-tracking.html\">Production tracking streamlining</a></h3><p>Nov 2025 · Publishing house. This showed how much operational waste can sit between source information and a usable reporting view. Build: 4 hours. Saves: 14 days/year.</p><h3><a href=\"/pages/project-fundraising-small-charity.html\">Fundraising automation</a></h3><p>Dec 2025 · Small local charity. A lighter intervention, but another confirmation that repeated paperwork can consume a surprising amount of useful time. Build: 0 hours. Saves: 5.5 days/year.</p><h3><a href=\"/pages/project-sales-data.html\">Sales data restructuring</a></h3><p>Dec 2025 · Charitable enterprise. This made the reporting layer usable again and reinforced that structure matters as much as automation. Build: 20 hours. Saves: 14 days/year.</p><h3><a href=\"/pages/project-till-data.html\">Till data automation</a></h3><p>Dec 2025 · Charitable enterprise. This added another pattern: repeatable source data and repeatable output handling are where the time disappears. Build: 7 hours. Saves: 11.5 days/year.</p><h3><a href=\"/pages/project-po-workflow-platform.html\">Bosonic PO workflow platform</a></h3><p>Feb 2026 · Bosonic. The most recent system pulled approvals, documents, routing, and auditability into one flow for Bosonic, strengthening the case for productising repeated business workflows. Build: 36 hours. Outcome: end-to-end PO workflow.</p><h2>If these patterns look familiar, go to the product pages next.</h2><h3><a href=\"/pages/services.html\">See the main workflow page</a></h3><p>Workflows. Best for site visits, inspections, maintenance work, compliance paperwork, document-heavy handoffs, and repeated operational admin.</p><h3><a href=\"/pages/demo.html\">Try the live demo</a></h3><p>See a workflow turn WhatsApp replies into a structured record in about two minutes.</p><h3><a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask about one repeated workflow</a></h3><p>Contact. The best enquiries describe one repeated job, report, approval flow, or paperwork pack and why it is still painful now.</p><h2>Need the same kind of reporting fix?</h2><p>If your team has the same repeated field-to-report pattern, get in touch. These examples are here to support a buying decision, not to turn the site into a browsing exercise.</p>",
